Subject: [PATCH] Search listings is the map: Zillow's layout, a
 describe-your-home search, and its Filters window

The map page fills the screen under the header: the map and two columns of homes, the MLS
notice once at the end of the list, no footer to scroll into. A click on a blue dot shows the
home on a card away from the dot; a second click opens it. Moving or zooming the map shows only
the homes with a dot in view, then searches that area. 2D or 3D in the top right, with the same
dots and list. On a phone: the list first and a Map button, like Zillow's app. Hearts save for
a signed-in visitor, on every card, also those drawn later.

The search box reads a sentence ("Describe your home in a sentence"): "fixer upper", "rental
homes", "single story", "near the beach", "nurse at McLeod" (10 miles, about 20 minutes) and more
become filters, shown as chips that remove themselves. Words the rules cannot read go once to
the AI, which picks from the CRM's own filter list; the answer is cached per sentence.

The bar: For sale, Price, Beds & baths, Home type, Filters. Filters opens a window like Zillow's:
the home first, then See more for the listing, commute and places (two pickers built from the
CRM's distance filters) and the area, then every MLS feature. Every guide's strip carries the same
bar, scaled down; a search there opens the map with it. The jobs guide asks "Name your industry
or employer to see homes nearby" and its strip is "Homes nearby major employers", within 10
miles. The header's Search Listings, the homepage button and the menu open the map.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
